# Target C-II — a near-quarter reflection braid can be forced through an `o(D)` central core

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the shallow-tooth reflection branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** Type-II full-core shallow-tooth reflection branch  
**Dependencies:** reflection/perimeter collapse; flat-cap dispersion; same-center source-hull disk sandwich; fixed-slack midpoint/star bounds; quarter-power reflection braid; shallow-tooth penetration matching  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_slow_scale_central_braid_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The fixed-width quarter-power braid and the preceding `D^(1/4)/log D` central braid
are two points of one scale-free statement.  Let

\[
\kappa_D\to\infty,
\qquad
\kappa_D=o(D^{1/4}),
\tag{SCB.1}
\]

and put

\[
w_D:=\kappa_DD^{-1/4}.
\tag{SCB.2}
\]

At the near-optimal fixed tooth threshold

\[
\eta={3\over26},
\tag{SCB.3}
\]

the reflection branch contains a family `B_D` of pairwise vertex-disjoint,
pairwise properly crossing reflection blockers satisfying

\[
\boxed{
|B_D|>
(c_*-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D},
\qquad
c_*:=0.129220199818177\ldots .
}
\tag{SCB.4}
\]

Every pairwise crossing point of this family lies in one common disk

\[
\boxed{
B\!\left(o,O\!\left({D\over\kappa_D}\right)\right),
}
\tag{SCB.5}
\]

so the crossing core is `o(D)`.

The full five-point tagged gadgets may again be retained pairwise vertex-disjoint: one
loaded base, one shallow apex, one penetration endpoint, and one reflection-blocker
head for every edge of `B_D`.

For example, taking

\[
\kappa_D=\log\log D
\]

(for `D` sufficiently large) gives

\[
\boxed{
|B_D|>
(0.129220199818177\ldots-o(1))
{D^{1/4}\over\log\log D}
}
\tag{SCB.6}
\]

with every mutual crossing in a disk of radius `O(D/log log D)` about the common
circumcenter.  This strictly improves the earlier logarithmic loss while preserving a
sublinear common crossing core.

More abstractly, choosing any subpolynomial divergent `kappa_D=D^{o(1)}` gives an

\[
D^{1/4-o(1)}
\]
full-gadget crossing braid whose entire crossing set is `o(D)`-central.

---

## 1. Fixed `eta=3/26` ledger

The current two-edge tooth and outer-detour bounds give, for every fixed `eta`,

\[
{N_{sh}(\eta)\over D}
\ge
0.4363893718701161781\ldots
-{0.002883975\over\eta}
-o(1).
\tag{SCB.7}
\]

At

\[
\eta={3\over26}
\]
this becomes

\[
\boxed{
U_D>(u_*-o(1))D,
\qquad
u_*=0.411394921870116\ldots .
}
\tag{SCB.8}
\]

after the perimeter-collapse deletion of the `o(D)` exterior-witness teeth.

Every selected base has length at least

\[
s_*=2-\eta={49\over26},
\tag{SCB.9}
\]

and the reflection slack is fixed below one:

\[
C_*^2=4\eta-\eta^2={303\over676}<1.
\tag{SCB.10}
\]

Thus all fixed-slack midpoint and star estimates apply uniformly.

The leading extraction coefficient below is

\[
\boxed{
{u_*s_*\over6}
=u_*{49\over156}
=0.129220199818177\ldots .
}
\tag{SCB.11}
\]

---

## 2. Flat-cap bin capacity at an arbitrary slow scale

As in the flat-cap reflection theorem, delete the `o(D)` blocked bases whose exterior
source-hull cap excess is not `o(1)`.  For every remaining base, if `S_D` is the
common-center tangential localization quantity, then

\[
S_D=O(D^{3/4}).
\tag{SCB.12}
\]

For one inward-normal bin `I` of width `w_D`, the boundary-projection estimate is

\[
N_I
\le
{1\over s_*}
{2S_D+2R_+\sin(w_D/2)\over\cos(w_D/2)}.
\tag{SCB.13}
\]

Because `kappa_D->infinity`, (SCB.12) gives

\[
2S_D=o(\kappa_DD^{3/4}).
\]

Because `kappa_D=o(D^{1/4})`, one has `w_D->0`, and therefore

\[
2R_+\sin(w_D/2)
=\left({1\over2}+o(1)\right)
\kappa_DD^{3/4}.
\]

Using `s_*=49/26`,

\[
\boxed{
N_I
\le
\left({13\over49}+o(1)\right)
\kappa_DD^{3/4}.
}
\tag{SCB.14}
\]

No rate for the pre-existing `o(1)` cap errors is required; the explicit divergent
factor `kappa_D` absorbs the fixed `O(D^(3/4))` localization term.

---

## 3. Dispersion produces `D^(1/4)/kappa_D` separated blockers

One normal quadrant contains at least

\[
\left({u_*\over4}-o(1)\right)D
\]

flat blocked bases.  Partition it into consecutive `w_D` bins.  Equation (SCB.14)
forces at least

\[
\left(
{u_*/4\over13/49}-o(1)
\right){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
\]

occupied bins.

Retain the largest residue class modulo three and choose one tooth from each retained
occupied bin.  Their inward normals are pairwise separated by at least `2w_D`, and the
selected population is

\[
\boxed{
|B_D|>
\left(u_*{49\over156}-o(1)\right)
{D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}.
}
\tag{SCB.15}

This proves the count (SCB.4).

---

## 4. Direction errors and shared endpoints disappear relative to `w_D`

For every flat selected base, the earlier same-center argument gives

\[
\angle(d_i,n_i)=O(D^{-1/4}),
\tag{SCB.16}
\]

where `n_i` is the inward base normal and `d_i` is the oriented reflection-blocker
direction.  The fixed-slack star theorem likewise says that two blockers sharing a
source endpoint have projective direction gap only

\[
O(D^{-1/4}).
\tag{SCB.17}
\]

But

\[
{D^{-1/4}\over w_D}={1\over\kappa_D}\to0.
\]

Therefore the selected blocker lines have pairwise projective angle

\[
\boxed{
\phi_{ij}\ge(2-o(1))w_D
}
\tag{SCB.18}
\]

and no two selected blockers can share a source endpoint.  They are pairwise
vertex-disjoint.

---

## 5. Every pairwise crossing lies in one `o(D)` disk

Let `m_i,m_j` be the midpoints of two selected blocker edges.  Fixed-slack midpoint
localization gives

\[
|m_i-o|,|m_j-o|=O(D^{3/4}),
\qquad
|m_i-m_j|=O(D^{3/4}).
\tag{SCB.19}
\]

Let `z_ij` be the intersection of their supporting lines.  From (SCB.18),

\[
|z_{ij}-m_i|,
|z_{ij}-m_j|
\le
{O(D^{3/4})\over\sin\phi_{ij}}
=O\!\left({D\over\kappa_D}\right).
\tag{SCB.20}
\]

Since `kappa_D=o(D^(1/4))`, the right side dominates the `O(D^(3/4))` midpoint radius,
so

\[
\boxed{
|z_{ij}-o|
=O\!\left({D\over\kappa_D}\right)
=o(D).
}
\tag{SCB.21}
\]

Every blocker has half-length `(1/2-o(1))D`.  Equation (SCB.20) is `o(D)`, hence
`z_ij` lies in the relative interior of both finite blocker segments.  With endpoint
disjointness, every intersection is proper.

This proves both pairwise crossing and the common-core statement (SCB.5).

---

## 6. The complete tooth gadgets remain disjoint

The quarter-power fixed-width theorem already isolated the only possible cross-gadget
collisions.  Its arguments become easier here because the selected normal gap is
`2kappa_DD^-1/4`, which dominates every `O(D^-1/4)` radial/base-normal error.

Thus:

1. two selected loaded bases cannot share a loaded endpoint;
2. the penetration-matching theorem gives distinct shallow apexes `x_i`, distinct deep
   endpoints `w_i`, and `X cap W=emptyset`;
3. every reflection head `y_j` is at distance `D-O(1)` from `x_j`, whereas all local
   vertices of another selected gadget are at distance at most
   `(1/sqrt(2)+o(1))D+O(1)` from `x_j`, because all selected radial directions lie in
   one quadrant.

Therefore the five-point sets

\[
\{a_i,b_i,x_i,w_i,y_i\}
\]

are pairwise disjoint throughout the family (SCB.15).

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The reflection branch now admits a tunable normal form:

```text
choose any kappa_D -> infinity with kappa_D=o(D^(1/4))
 -> (c_*-o(1)) D^(1/4)/kappa_D pairwise-disjoint full tooth gadgets
 -> their D-O(1) reflection blockers cross pairwise
 -> every crossing occurs in one O(D/kappa_D) disk about the common center.
```

Thus the previous `log D` loss is not structural.  One may retain an
`D^(1/4-o(1))` approximate thrackle while forcing the entire crossing set into an
`o(D)` common core.

This still does not close C-II: a near-circular source can support many almost-radial
fixed-slack chords through a sublinear central region.  The remaining useful bridge is
now more specific.  It must exploit the attached ordered, nonadjacent loaded bases and
penetration contacts, not merely the crossing geometry of the long blockers.  A
simultaneous discrete reflection or a width/turn inequality coupling those local tooth
gadgets across the central braid would close this survivor.

**Disposition:** generalized the reflection-braid extraction to an arbitrary slow
inverse-quarter-power scale, yielding `D^(1/4-o(1))` pairwise-crossing full tooth
gadgets with all blocker crossings confined to an `o(D)` central core.  C-II and Level
I remain open.
